 "details": {
  "title": "You’d Be So Nice to Come Home To, on the Fret Finder guitar",
  "lead": "The tune for trumpet, set on the bars of the Fret Finder guitar arrangement, which follows a slow record in A minor, the lead sheet’s own key: concert A4 to E5 (B to F♯ for the trumpet). The record’s arranger stretches the song, so the lead sheet’s bars sit where the singer on that record puts them: a note lasts longer where he holds it, the pickups come where his do, and where the band’s chord comes half a bar early the note comes with it. The notes are the lead sheet’s. The long free intro and the instrumental, where the record has a horn solo, are the guitar’s; the trumpet comes back with the pickup. At the end it plays the record’s last horn line, as the guitar plays it on the page, and holds the C.",
  "facts": [
   [
    "Song",
    "Cole Porter (1942)"
   ],
   [
    "Line",
    "The tune from the lead sheet, on the Fret Finder page’s bars and chords; the last line is the record’s horn, as the page has it"
   ],
   [
    "Key",
    "A minor concert (B minor for the trumpet)"
   ],
   [
    "Form",
    "Intro (guitar), the song’s first half, its second half, instrumental (guitar), the second half again, ending"
   ],
   [
